titre$Key to the Clavarioid Families of Homobasidiomycetes
com$Corner 1970 SUPPLEMENT TO "AMONOGRAPH OF CLAVARIA AND ALLIED GENERA" / Adapt GF mai 2012

1,0$  Dimitic with colourless skeletal hyphae; generative hyphae clamped or not, not inflating; spores white, smooth, not amyloid; fruit-bodies filiform or with filiform branches, erect, deflexed, or decumbent $ Pterulaceae, p. 194
1,2$  Without such character(= not "Dimitic with colourless skeletal hyphae; generative hyphae clamped or not, not inflating; spores white, smooth, not amyloid; fruit-bodies filiform or with filiform branches, erect, deflexed, or decumbent ")
2,3$  Xanthochroic; hyphae without clamps, not inflating, monomitic or with dichophyses; spores white, smooth, not amyloid; fruit-bodies erect.
2,4$  Not xanthochroic; dichophyses and brown setae absent.
3,0$  Setae absent; gloeocystidia and dichophyses present $ Lachnocladiaceae, p. 178
3,0$  Setae present, brown; gloeocystidia and dichophyses absent , $ Hymenochaetaceae, p. 176
4,5$  Gloeocystidia usually present; spores white, mostly amyloid, smooth or asperulate, not angled. 
4,6$  Gloeocystidia absent.
5,0$  Erect, usually pyxidately branched .. , $ Clavicoronaceae, p. 143
5,0$  Hydnoid, spines as deflexed branches, or the minute fruit-bodies pendent (with or without gloeocystidia) $ Hericiaceae, p. 170
6,7$  Spores with coloured walls, not amyloid; fruit-bodies erect, branched.
6,8$  Spores white (or with pink to yellowish contents).
7,0$  Spores and hyphae with fuscous, dull brown or purplish brown walls; spores echinulate, verrucose or lobulate, often angular; hyphae rarely inflated; tissue often cyanescent in potash, usually tough; branching flattened $ Thelephoraceae, p. 11
7,0$  Spores with ochraceous to ferruginous brown walls, often ornamented, not angular; hyphae inflating, with colourless walls; tissue usually fleshy; branching rarely flattened , $ Ramariaceae, p. 224
8,0$  Spores subglobose with amyloid spines and cristations; hyphae dimitic with skeletals, not inflating; branching flattened $ Amylariaceae, p. 15
8,9$  Spores not amyloid or, if so, then smooth and ellipsoid; hyphae monomitic.
9,0$  Young basidia subacerose; hymenium usually with oleocystidia; fruit-bodies small, simple, with distinct head and stalk, variously orientated $ Physalacriaceae, p. 187
9,10$  Young basidia cylindric to subclavate; fruit-bodies generally erect.
10,11$  Hyphae not inflated; fruit-body erect and branched or corticoid with spines; spores smooth.
10,13$  Hyphae inflating or, if not, then the fruit-bodies minute, filiform and simple (Ceratelloplis) or with echinulate spores (Scytinopogon).
11,0$  Basidia simple $ Aphelariaceae, p. 15
11,0$  Basidia large, sulcately divided at the apex by the large sterigmata $Tremellodendropsis, p. 283
13,0$  Sterigmata curved, 2 (if more, then the hyphae with thick brown walls); basidia often transversely septate at maturity $ Clavulinaceae, p. 149
13,14$  Sterigmata straight.
14,0$  Spores smooth or echinulate with l-several guttae, or the hyphae secondarily septate without clamps; fruit-bodies branched or simple . $ Clavariaceae, p. 23
14,15$  Spore-contents multiguttulate to opalescent or without guttulae. 
15,0$  Fruit-bodies branched or odontioid; hyphae with thick or thin walls $ Amariaceae, p. 224
15,0$  Fruit-bodies simple, massive to minute, the smaller epiphytic species with agglutinated hyphae on the stem-surface; medunary hyphae thin-walled $ Clavariadelphaceae, p. 93
